    {
      "id": "p0030-b006",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "A literal translation can leave an unintended impression if it sounds funny or inappropriate in the target language. For example, there's an item in the Pokemon series that restores Power Points. In Japanese, the item is literally called the \"PP Aid\". This name would sound unintentionally funny and inappropriate in English, however, so the item was renamed to something tamer during localization.",
        "raw": "A literal translation can leave an unintended impression if it sounds funny\nor inappropriate in the target language. For example, there's an item in the\nPokemon series that restores Power Points. In Japanese, the item is literally\ncalled the \"PP Aid\". This name would sound unintentionally funny and\ninappropriate in English, however, so the item was renamed to something\ntamer during localization.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29348251,
          0.58478571,
          0.87076471,
          0.68932857
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.29118335,
          0.58407143,
          0.90430007,
          0.6904
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          20,
          22,
          23,
          24,
          25,
          26
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "直译若在目标语言里听着滑稽或不妥，就会留下意外的印象。例如《Pokémon》系列有一种恢复招式点数的道具，日语名称直译是“PP Aid”（PP辅助剂）。但这个名字在英语里听着意外地滑稽、不雅（PP 与 pee-pee“尿尿”同音），所以本地化时改成了更稳妥的名字。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
