    {
      "id": "p0030-b008",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "A literal translation of an idea that's clearly defined in the source language can become ambiguous in the target language. For example, the Japanese term hikari dameji literally means \"light damage\" in English. But wait, does that mean \"damage that isn't severe\" or does it mean \"damage from light beams\"? In Japanese, it's clearly the latter, but the literal English translation paves the way for confusion.",
        "raw": "A literal translation of an idea that's clearly defined in the source language can\nbecome ambiguous in the target language. For example, the Japanese term\nhikari dameji literally means \"light damage\" in English. But wait, does that\nmean \"damage that isn't severe\" or does it mean \"damage from light beams\"?\nIn Japanese, it's clearly the latter, but the literal English translation paves the\nway for confusion.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29444471,
          0.73192143,
          0.89376646,
          0.83647857
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.29118335,
          0.73120714,
          0.90430007,
          0.83755
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          30,
          32,
          34,
          36,
          37,
          38
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "原语言里清楚明确的概念，直译后可能产生歧义。例如日语 hikari damēji（光属性伤害），直译成英语是 light damage。但等等：是“轻微伤害”，还是“光束造成的伤害”？日语明确是后者，英语直译却给误解留下了空间。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
          {
            "start": 27,
            "end": 40,
            "italic": true,
            "lang": "en"
          }
        ]
